  "image_name": "526805565b2bb96bc716aad8c3afbf12.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a synthesis of different large-scale slope-failure styles shown as simplified cross sections. It groups examples into rotational slides, compound or planar block slides, rotational slides mixed with lateral spreading, rotational slides combined with rock avalanches, and pure lateral spreads. Across the panels, strong rock units such as limestone, sandstone, or basalt are drawn as large blue blocks resting on weaker yellow materials, emphasizing that failure commonly occurs where competent caprock overlies weak strata.\n\nThe main message is that slope collapse can follow several geometries depending on structure and topography. Some failures are deep-seated and curved, with back-tilted blocks and head scarps indicating rotational motion. Others move more along flatter or stepped slip surfaces, breaking into coherent blocks. In several examples, downslope movement evolves into lateral spreading, where the upper rock mass extends and fragments over a weak substrate. A few panels show collapse transforming into rock avalanches, with debris spreading far beyond the source area. Green deposits indicate younger sediments or post-landslide debris that partly fill valleys or coastal zones. Overall, the figure highlights how rock strength contrasts, bedding, and landscape setting control landslide form and runout.",
